<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Early Ly dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">544 CE – 602 CE · capital Long Bien</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first native Vietnamese dynasty to break from Chinese rule, founded by Ly Bi, who declared himself emperor of Van Xuan in 544.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 541 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-nam-de">Ly Bi</a>, a local landowner of mixed Chinese and Vietnamese descent, led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a>'s governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiao-province">Jiaozhou</a>. By 544 he had driven out <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> officials and proclaimed himself Ly Nam De, emperor of the south, naming the state Van Xuan, 'ten thousand springs'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-nam-de">Ly Nam De</a> set up a court on Chinese lines, with an era name and officials, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-quoc-pagoda">Khai Quoc</a> pagoda near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-bien">Long Bien</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-chen">Chen Baxian</a> defeated him in 545, but resistance continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieu-quang-phuc">Trieu Quang Phuc</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-phat-tu">Ly Phat Tu</a>, who held the delta for decades through a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare and negotiation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 602 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> sent an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-fang">Liu Fang</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-phat-tu">Ly Phat Tu</a> surrendered, returning the region to Chinese control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-nam-de">Ly Bi</a>'s revolt is celebrated in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> as an early assertion of independence, and Van Xuan was the first name for the country chosen by its own rulers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/early-ly-dynasty.jpg" alt="Early Ly dynasty" width="320" height="394"><figcaption>Image: I Love Triệu Đà · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_V%E1%BA%A1n_Xu%C3%A2n_Kingdom_during_Early_L%C3%BD_dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>544 CE – 602 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Long Bien</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ly Nam De, Trieu Viet Vuong, Ly Phat Tu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Chinese folk religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Vietnamese, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Vietnam</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Van Xuan, Vạn Xuân, Former Ly dynasty, Tiền Lý</dd></dl></aside></div>

